---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import shopPhoto from '../content/bikes/shop/Matt-Nate-BW.png';
---

<Layout title="Story" description="The story of Signal Cycles, 2007–2018.">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <h1>The Signal Cycles story</h1>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap story-layout">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Signal Cycles started in the fall of 2007 in a small garage in North
        Portland. Matt Cardinal and Nate Meschke met while working as bike mechanics
        at Bike Gallery. Both had earned undergraduate painting degrees: Nate at the
        University of Nebraska–Lincoln, and Matt at Pacific Northwest College of Art
        in Portland. Signal debuted at the North American Handmade Bicycle Show in
        February of 2008 and began filling orders immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        Signal was included in a book called <em>Custom Bicycles: A Passionate
        Pursuit</em>, alongside established builders like Richard Sachs, Dario
        Pegoretti, and Vanilla Bicycles, with a foreword by cycling legend Phil
        Liggett.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over the next decade Signal built steel bicycles, one at a time, to the
        highest standard that Matt and Nate could manage. Bikes shipped all over the
        US, and to Europe, China, and Japan. Signal was featured in the magazine <em>ReadyMade</em>,
        built a bike for the 7-foot Portland Trail Blazers center Joel Przybilla, and built custom picnic bicycles for the Anthropologie
        clothing store. Signal won Best City Bike at the 2011 NAHBS show, supported a
        cyclocross team with competitive men's and women's racers, and was selected to
        work with Ziba design studio on the Oregon Manifest creative collaborations.
        All the while, Nate and Matt kept getting better at indoor nerf basketball.
      </p>
      <p>These were very good years, full of creativity, friendships, and learning.</p>
      <p>
        In the spring of 2013 Matt left to pursue a master's degree in industrial
        design in Savannah, Georgia. Nate kept building and eventually stopped in
        2018.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is a record of what Signal built: the beautiful bicycles and
        amazing relationships that were created and crafted in North Portland, one at
        a time.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="story-photo">
      <Image
        src={shopPhoto}
        alt="Matt Cardinal and Nate Meschke in the Signal Cycles workshop"
        width={800}
        sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 400px"
      />
    </figure>
  </div>
</Layout>
